<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl Peters</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl Peters was a German colonial explorer and the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa-company">German East Africa Company</a>. In 1884 he made treaties with chiefs inland from the Zanzibar coast, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> placed under imperial protection in 1885. Later an imperial commissioner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro">Kilimanjaro</a>, he was dismissed in 1897 for his brutal treatment of Africans, including executions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-peters.jpg" alt="Carl Peters" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R30019,_Dr._Carl_Peters.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peters was born on 27 September 1856 at Neuhaus an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, the son of a Lutheran clergyman. He studied history and philosophy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he was a student of Heinrich von Treitschke, and wrote a prize-winning dissertation on the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of 1177. Rather than teach, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to stay with his uncle, the composer Carl Engel, refused an offer to become a British subject, and on his return to Berlin founded the Society for German Colonization.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The treaties he made in Usagara in 1884 were written in German and had little legal value; Karl Jühlke, a member of the expedition, conceded that the chiefs were probably not clear what they signed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> at first refused support, but Peters threatened to sell his gains to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-of-belgium">Leopold II of Belgium</a>, and the charter was granted on 27 February 1885. Peters had no business experience, and when the company was refinanced in 1887 he lost control of it.</p><p>In 1888 he led an expedition, avowedly to relieve Emin Pasha but in fact to extend German influence into Uganda, seizing food from the local people; the Danish explorer Peder Scavenius later found burnt villages and skeletons along its route. His treaty with Kabaka Mwanga II of Buganda was voided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heligoland-zanzibar-treaty">Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</a> of 1 July 1890. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reichskommissar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro">Kilimanjaro</a> from 1891 he had his servant Mabruk hanged and, in January 1892, Jagodjo, one of the African women the Germans kept as sex slaves, whom he suspected of spying. August Bebel made the killings public in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> on 13 March 1896.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Deprived of his commission and pension, Peters moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and explored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> region for a gold mining company, identifying the medieval mines of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> with the ancient land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ophir">Ophir</a>. In 1914 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> restored his title and gave him a pension. He published an autobiography in February 1918 and died on 10 September that year.</p><p>A proponent of Social Darwinism and Völkisch thought, he was called Hänge-Peters (Hangman-Peters) by critics in the German press and mkono wa damu, the man with blood on his hands, by Africans. Colonial circles hailed him as a national hero, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> rehabilitated him by decree in 1936, and a 1941 propaganda film starred Hans Albers as Peters. Many German streets named after him were later renamed, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>'s Petersallee on 23 August 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carl Peters” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony began with the treaties that <b>Carl Peters</b>, an adventurer and the founder of the Society for German Colonization, signed with several chieftains on the mainland opposite Zanzibar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Carl Peters</b> made treaties with chiefs in the interior in 1884, and the German naval demonstration of 1885 forced Barghash to recognise them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1884 <b>Carl Peters</b> made treaties with chiefs inland from the Zanzibar coast, and in 1885 the German East Africa Company received an imperial charter.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Neuhaus, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Bad Harzburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.29,10.93&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Neuhaus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4794 330 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carl Peters"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5244 600h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5244 600h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.29,10.93&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
